# LW-WATCHTOWER keeps no mutable state in the repo - state lives in
# $CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA (default ~/.claude/plugins/data/lw-watchtower/).
# Anything matching below in a working tree is a bug, not an artifact.
# That directory was ~/.claude/plugins/data/lw-gmhh/ before the 3 August 2026
# rename; nothing migrates it - see `## [0.4.0]` in CHANGELOG.md.
*.jsonl
*.probe
state/
data/
logs/

# Claude Code's per-checkout directory. NOT ignored wholesale on purpose: a
# shared .claude/settings.json or .claude/agents/ is a legitimate thing to
# track, and a blanket ".claude/" would hide it from git status forever.
# Only the two machine-local things that actually appear here are named.
#
# .claude/worktrees/ is where agent worktrees are checked out - each is a
# full second copy of this repo, complete with its own .git file. A
# "git add -A" run while agents are working would commit the repo into
# itself, so this line is the one that has to be right.
.claude/worktrees/
# settings.local.json is a per-machine permission grant. Already covered by
# *.local.json below; named here so the intent survives an edit to that rule.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Per-machine worker facts. context/worker_facts.md is TRACKED and therefore
# ships identically to every install, so it may only state invariants; anything
# true of one laptop only - an interpreter path, what resolves on PATH, the
# local agent-name prefix, an owner's push preference - goes here instead, and
# lib/subagent_start.ps1 appends it after the invariants when it exists. Named
# as a full path rather than a *.local.md glob so that a deliberately tracked
# .local.md elsewhere in the tree is not swallowed by it.
context/worker_facts.local.md

# credentials. This block is PROPHYLAXIS, NOT REMEDIATION, and it is worth
# saying which it is. The pre-publication audit reported that it built the full
# historical path set from the remote's whole reachable history - 110 paths -
# and that none of these rules matched any of it. That measurement is QUOTED
# rather than re-run here, so read it as "the audit found nothing", not as "this
# comment checked". What was measured directly on 3 August 2026 is the working
# tree: `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all` carries nothing matching
# any rule below.
#
# It is here because when it was written the three layers that would normally
# make it unnecessary were absent at once: GitHub secret scanning and push
# protection were OFF and were not even settable on the plan and visibility then
# in force, and the secret_scan module that used to inspect writes was removed on
# 30 July 2026. TWO OF THE THREE CAME BACK ON 2026-08-28. The repository went
# public that day and secret scanning and push protection were switched on -
# read from `gh api repos/LEAPWare-Software/LEAPWare-Watchtower` under
# security_and_analysis, both `enabled`. The secret_scan module is still gone and
# is not coming back. KEEP THIS FILE ANYWAY, and the reason is not inertia: push
# protection fires at the push, on the server, after the secret is already in a
# local commit and in the reflog, and it knows only the patterns GitHub ships.
# These rules stop the file being staged at all, on the machine, for path shapes
# GitHub has no pattern for. The two catch different things at different moments,
# which is why one is not a substitute for the other.
#
# THE LIST IS NOT INVENTED. It is the secret-path set the removed gate matched
# on, kept in tests/fixtures/deny_canonical.txt. That fixture was deleted with
# tests/deny_parity.ps1 on the same day and this comment said so; it was
# RESTORED to the tree from ef993bc on 3 August 2026 as the subject of a
# deletion case, so it is readable again and the claim below is now checkable.
#
# IT DOES NOT CHECK OUT, AND THAT IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE FOUND.
# This comment used to end "Reusing the project's own considered answer keeps
# the two consistent if that list is ever revived." Compared line by line on
# 3 August 2026 the two DIVERGE in both directions: the fixture refuses
# id_dsa*, id_ecdsa* and hosts.yml, none of which the rules below ignore, and
# the rules below ignore *.pfx and *.p12, which the fixture never named. The
# divergence is not closed here. Two of the three are plain SSH private keys
# and closing those is a defensible one-line change; hosts.yml is not, for the
# same reason secrets.json is not - see the block below - and an ignore rule
# that appears because a historical fixture was restored is a rule nobody
# decided on. What this comment can honestly claim is narrower than what it
# claimed: the list has a considered ORIGIN, not a maintained equality.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it names file CLASSES, not secrets. A token pasted into a
# tracked .md, a key in a file called notes.txt, or a credential inside a
# .json that is not *.local.json all walk straight past it. An ignore file
# cannot read content, so nothing here should be read as "a credential cannot
# be committed from this tree".
#
# TWO NAMES FROM THE ORIGINAL MEASUREMENT ARE STILL TRACKABLE ON PURPOSE, and
# they are named here rather than left for someone to discover: `secrets.json`
# and `creds.txt`. Neither was in the removed gate's list, both are ordinary
# enough words that a rule for them would hide a legitimately tracked file, and
# a `git check-ignore secrets.json` today still answers "not ignored". If that
# is not acceptable, add the rule deliberately - do not assume this block
# already did.
.env
# .env.* with the negation, and not a bare .env, because someone will want to
# track a template later. Without the exception that costs a `git add -f`, and
# what people actually do then is delete the ignore rule.
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.credentials.json
.npmrc

# local scratch
*.tmp
*.bak
*.local.json
.junction-probe.tmp

# editors / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
